---
title: Add an instrument or a sound bank
description: Add your own samples to the game by adding an instrument to a sound bank, or by creating a new bank.
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

A **sound bank** holds up to 16 instruments. Each instrument is a mono WAV sample plus its tuning, an optional loop, and an envelope that shapes its volume. Sound effects, ambient sequences, and songs play samples through the instruments in banks.

In this guide, you add a new sound bank with one instrument and then play it from a sound effect. Later sections show how to add an instrument to one of the game's banks and how to replace a sample.

## Prepare the WAV file

Export your sample with these settings:

- Mono
- 16-bit PCM, uncompressed
- Any sample rate. The build reads the rate from the file, and lower rates make smaller ROM files.

Name the file after the sample, in snake case, such as `chime.wav`. The sample name must be unique across all banks.

## Choose a bank

You have two options:

- **Create a new bank.** Your bank has its own folder and doesn't touch the game's banks. Choose this unless you have a reason not to.
- **Add to an existing bank.** You ship a changed `sound_bank.xml` and your new WAV file. You don't copy the bank's other samples. See [Add an instrument to a game bank](#add-an-instrument-to-a-game-bank).

The engine loads banks in two ways:

| Kind | How you set it up | When it's loaded |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Always-loaded | List the bank in `audio/banks.xml` | When the game starts |
| Song bank | Name the bank in `bk1`, `bk2`, or `bk3` of a `Song` entry, without listing it in `audio/banks.xml` | When that song loads |

Sound effects and ambient sequences look up samples in the always-loaded banks, so a sample must be in one of those banks to play from them. Use a song bank for samples that only one song needs.

## Create a bank

1. Create the folder `audio/bank/MOD1`. The folder name is the bank name, and it can have four characters at most.

2. Copy your WAV file into the folder as `audio/bank/MOD1/chime.wav`.

3. Create `audio/bank/MOD1/sound_bank.xml`.

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SoundBank>
	<Instruments>
		<Instrument wav="chime" envName="env1" keyBase="4800"/>
	</Instruments>
	<Envelopes>
		<Envelope envName="env1">
			<Commands>
				<Press>
					<Point duration="0" volume="7F"/>
					<End/>
				</Press>
				<Release>
					<Point duration="120ms" volume="00"/>
					<End/>
				</Release>
			</Commands>
		</Envelope>
	</Envelopes>
</SoundBank>
```

The `Instrument` attributes are:

| Attribute | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `wav` | The file name without `.wav`. Sound effects, ambient sequences, and songs use this name to refer to the instrument. |
| `envName` | An envelope in the same file. `env1` holds the volume at full while a note is on and fades it out in 120 milliseconds. |
| `keyBase` | The pitch at which the sample plays unchanged, in cents (hundredths of a note). `4800` is note 48, which is the default pitch of a one-shot sound effect. Required. |
| `loop`, `loopCount` | Optional. See [Loop a sample](#loop-a-sample). |

The order of instruments is their index in the bank, so add new instruments at the end of an existing bank.

4. Create `audio/banks.xml` and list the bank as always loaded.

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<BankList>
	<Bank name="MOD1.bk"/>
</BankList>
```

The build puts the bank in the first free slot. To choose the slot yourself, add both `group` (from `2` to `6`) and `index` (from `0` to `F`). The engine loads at most 79 always-loaded banks.

5. Rebuild the ROM. The build log shows the encoding step.

```text
encoded MOD1/chime: new codebook
```

That line means the build compressed your sample into the game's audio format.

6. Use the instrument in a sound effect. Add this entry to `audio/sound_effects.xml`, as described in [Add a sound effect](/papermario-dx/audio/sound-effects).

```xml
	<Sound name="ShopBell">
		<Routing allocation="dynamic" maxPlayer="7" priority="0"/>
		<OneShot wav="chime" volume="70" lockVolume="true" lockPitch="true"/>
	</Sound>
```

Rebuild, play `SOUND_SHOP_BELL` in the game, and check that you hear your sample. The Audio Booth in [Star Rod](/papermario-dx/star-rod) also previews samples.

## Add an instrument to a game bank

Each file in a bank resolves on its own, so a bank in your layer needs only the files you add or change. The game's other samples stay exactly as they are.

1. Copy the game's `audio/bank/SYS2/sound_bank.xml` to `audio/bank/SYS2/sound_bank.xml` in your layer. Your copy replaces the game's file, so it must list every instrument of the bank. Keep the `Encoding` element of each existing instrument.

2. Add your instrument at the end of the `Instruments` element. The game's `SYS2` bank has 12 instruments, so it has room for four more.

```xml
		<Instrument wav="shop_bell" envName="env1" keyBase="4800"/>
```

3. Copy your WAV file to `audio/bank/SYS2/shop_bell.wav`.

4. Rebuild. Only the new sample is encoded, and the game's other samples in `SYS2` are unchanged.

Sound effects and ambient sequences can use the instrument if the bank is always loaded, as `SYS2` is.

## Replace a sample

To change how an existing sample sounds, copy your WAV file to the same path as the game's file, such as `audio/bank/SYS1/open_popup.wav`. You don't need a `sound_bank.xml` file.

The build keeps the encoding of every sample you didn't change. It encodes the replaced sample again and logs that it did:

```text
re-encoded SYS1/open_popup: new codebook
```

## Loop a sample

A looping instrument uses two WAV files: one for the attack and one that repeats, named `<sample>_loop.wav`. Both files must have the same sample rate.

```xml
<Instrument wav="hum" loop="hum_loop" envName="env1" keyBase="4800"/>
```

The loop repeats forever. To repeat it a fixed number of times, add `loopCount`. The build ignores loop points stored inside a WAV file, so trim the two files where you want the loop to start and end.

## Limits

- A bank holds 16 instruments at most.
- A sample name can appear in only one bank.
- A bank that a song loads with `bk1`, `bk2`, or `bk3` can hold fewer instruments and envelopes than an always-loaded bank. The build reports an error if the bank is too big.
- Your audio must fit in the space the engine can address. The build reports an error if it doesn't. Use shorter samples or lower sample rates.

